% A more complex abduction example, combining the ideas of PEG Lisbon 2026
% slides 24-26 and 28: differential diagnosis with several observations,
% several candidate causes, and constraint-style elimination of candidates.
%
% The candidate diseases are open (abducible): "*a person* has *a disease*"
% is declared "; assumable", so diseases are ASSUMED in order to explain the
% observed symptoms, and each answer's unknowns are one candidate diagnosis.
% The conjunctive observation "bob exhibits fever and bob exhibits rash" has
% four abductive explanations — including the single-cause one, {measles},
% which explains BOTH symptoms.
%
% Logical English has no integrity constraints, but the effect of slide 28's
% passive constraint ("it is not the case that the sprinkler was on if the
% sprinkler is broken") is obtained here with negation as failure over closed
% (scenario) facts: the rules that blame measles are guarded by "it is not
% the case that the person is immune to measles". In the checkup scenario
% nothing establishes immunity, so the guard succeeds and measles stays a
% candidate; in the vaccinated scenario the fact "bob is vaccinated against
% measles" derives immunity, the guard fails, and every explanation that
% assumes measles is eliminated — only {flu, food allergy} survives.
% (Note that negation attacks ASSUMPTIONS too: since the diseases are
% assumable, a guard such as "it is not the case that the person has measles"
% would never succeed. The guard must test a CLOSED predicate, which is why
% immunity is derived from the vaccination facts.)

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    *a person* exhibits *a symptom*.
    *a person* has *a disease*; assumable.
    *a person* is immune to *a disease*.
    *a person* is vaccinated against *a disease*.

the knowledge base diagnosis includes:

a person exhibits fever if
    the person has flu.

a person exhibits fever if
    the person has measles
    and it is not the case that the person is immune to measles.

a person exhibits rash if
    the person has measles
    and it is not the case that the person is immune to measles.

a person exhibits rash if
    the person has food allergy.

a person is immune to a disease if
    the person is vaccinated against the disease.

scenario checkup is:
    diagnose expects answers [
        "bob exhibits fever and bob exhibits rash",
        "bob exhibits fever and bob exhibits rash",
        "bob exhibits fever and bob exhibits rash",
        "bob exhibits fever and bob exhibits rash"
    ] and unknowns [
        "bob has flu",
        "bob has measles",
        "bob has food allergy"
    ].
    fever expects answers [
        "bob exhibits fever",
        "bob exhibits fever"
    ] and unknowns [
        "bob has flu",
        "bob has measles"
    ].

scenario vaccinated is:
    bob is vaccinated against measles.
    diagnose expects answers [
        "bob exhibits fever and bob exhibits rash"
    ] and unknowns [
        "bob has flu",
        "bob has food allergy"
    ].
    fever expects answers [
        "bob exhibits fever"
    ] and unknowns [
        "bob has flu"
    ].

query diagnose is:
    bob exhibits fever and bob exhibits rash.

query fever is:
    bob exhibits fever.
